    description: "REPRODUCED ON A CLEAN TREE. Not caused by the change that found it.\n\n  $ git stash          # working tree at dev f70cf62fa2, no local edits\n  $ python -m pytest packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/ \\\n      packages/hypergumbo-lang-common/tests/ packages/hypergumbo-lang-extended1/tests/ \\\n      packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/tests/ \\\n      packages/hypergumbo-lang-rust-analyzer/tests/ packages/hypergumbo/tests/ \\\n      packages/hypergumbo-tracker/tests/ -n 5 -q\n  2 failed, 22100 passed, 9 skipped, 17 xfailed\n\n  FAILED test_taint_recall_corpus.py::test_adjudication_label_does_not_depend_on_another_language\n  FAILED test_taint_recall_corpus.py::test_published_scope_distinguishes_capable_from_incapable\n\n  test_taint_recall_corpus.py:687:\n      assert by_lang[\"javascript\"][\"dataflow_capable\"] is True\n  E   assert False is True\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND A RED SUITE. `dataflow_capable` is the published INV-karud\nclause (a3) coverage signal -- the field that tells a reader whether \"structural\"\nmeans \"the walk ran and found nothing\" or \"nothing here was capable of looking\". A\ntest run in which JavaScript silently reads incapable is a run in which that signal\nis wrong, and the same global state feeds the real `dataflow_coverage` block. The\nfailing tests are the ones written to guard exactly that.\n\nWHAT IS ESTABLISHED:\n  * Pre-existing at f70cf62fa2. Verified by stashing all local edits and re-running.\n  * Order/co-residency dependent, not a flake. It reproduced on 3 of 3 full runs and\n    passes 3 of 3 times when test_taint_recall_corpus.py runs alone, INCLUDING with\n    -n 5, so parallelism alone is not the trigger -- it needs other tests resident in\n    the same worker process.\n  * CI does not currently surface it: ci-debug reports push + all four nightly runs\n    GREEN at f70cf62f, the same commit that fails locally under -n 5. So the trigger\n    is a worker-count / distribution property, and CI's configuration happens not to\n    produce the poisoning order. That makes this a latent gate hole, not just a\n    nuisance: the suite can go green while the property is violated.\n  * It surfaced when an unrelated PR added ~21 tests, which shifted xdist's\n    distribution. Nothing about that PR touched the DDG or taint registries.\n\nWHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED -- the mechanism. Do not act on the hypothesis below\nwithout confirming it; it is a hypothesis that a targeted probe already FAILED to\nreproduce.\n\n  HYPOTHESIS: test_ddg_build.py's `_preserve_registry` autouse fixture snapshots\n  `ddg_build._DDG_LANGUAGES`, then on teardown does `.clear()` + `.update(saved)`.\n  Registration happens as an IMPORT SIDE EFFECT\n  (dataflow_scope.ensure_def_use_extractors_registered imports\n  hypergumbo_lang_mainstream.ts_def_use). If a registration lands AFTER the snapshot\n  and BEFORE the teardown, the restore deletes it -- and it can never come back,\n  because sys.modules has the module cached so the re-import is a no-op and the\n  decorator never runs again. That shape would explain a permanent, one-way loss of\n  the `javascript` spec inside one worker.\n\n  WHY IT IS ONLY A HYPOTHESIS: running test_ddg_build.py together with\n  test_taint_recall_corpus.py in one process PASSES (20 passed, 1 xfailed), as does\n  the 4-file combination with test_cfg.py and test_cli_verify_claims_ddg.py (163\n  passed). So either the polluter is elsewhere, or it needs a third participant.\n  Localize it by bisecting the per-worker test list (pytest -n 5 with\n  `--dist loadfile` vs `load` will change the grouping and is a cheap first probe),\n  NOT by reasoning from the fixture alone.\n\nSUGGESTED DIRECTION IF THE HYPOTHESIS DOES HOLD: a registry whose contents come from\nimport side effects cannot be safely restored by snapshot, because the source of\ntruth (the decorator) only ever fires once per process. Either make registration\nidempotent and re-runnable (a `register_all()` the fixture calls instead of\n`update(saved)`), or stop clearing the shared dict and give the test its own\nregistry. The second is the structural fix; the first is a patch.\n\nNOT MEASURED: whether any OTHER published field is corrupted by the same leak; which\nworker count thresholds trigger it; whether CI's configuration is stably immune or\nmerely lucky today."  # d390
